IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- HELPERS FOR THE mlr2 PART (regs 33 to 45ZB of the Money Laundering, Terrorist Financing and Transfer of
-- Funds (Information on the Payer) Regulations 2017).  Three-valued logic over MAYBE BOOLEAN.
-- The text is a plain-text rendering made by a converter script; it is not the official text.
--
-- These are not provisions, so their names begin `mlr2 ` (the part's prefix) instead of a regulation
-- label.  No other part can define such a name.
--
-- THE THREE ANSWERS.  JUST TRUE and JUST FALSE are answers the Regulations give; NOTHING means the
-- Regulations do not answer on these facts.  A caller must never turn NOTHING into FALSE.
-- The tags below say why an answer is given: a FALSE-kind or NOTHING-kind comment on every branch.

-- Conjunction.  A conjunct answered "no" settles the whole as "no"; "yes" needs both; otherwise the
-- Regulations have not answered.
GIVEN first IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      second IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`mlr2 both are true` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF first EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the first conjunct was answered no by the text that supplied it): the answer is passed through, not decided here
        IF second EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the second conjunct was answered no by the text that supplied it): the answer is passed through, not decided here
        IF first EQUALS JUST TRUE AND second EQUALS JUST TRUE THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): at least one conjunct has not been answered

-- Disjunction.  A disjunct answered "yes" settles the whole as "yes"; "no" needs both; otherwise the
-- Regulations have not answered.
GIVEN first IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      second IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`mlr2 either is true` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF first EQUALS JUST TRUE THEN JUST TRUE
        IF second EQUALS JUST TRUE THEN JUST TRUE
        IF first EQUALS JUST FALSE AND second EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (both disjuncts were answered no by the text that supplied them): the answer is passed through, not decided here
        OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): at least one disjunct has not been answered

-- Negation.
GIVEN answer IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`mlr2 the opposite of` MEANS
    CONSIDER answer
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text that supplied the answer said yes): the opposite is passed through, not decided here
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the answer has not been given

-- A fact the caller states (a BOOLEAN) as an answer.
GIVEN fact IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`mlr2 the fact stated` MEANS
    IF fact THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the caller states that the fact does not hold): a stated fact, not a decision by the text

-- SEAM S-8 (asking), the shape every rule that begins "A relevant person ..." takes.  A rule addressed
-- to a relevant person answers only for one.  For anyone else the Regulations are silent: the rule
-- speaks of "a relevant person", and says nothing of a person who is not one.
GIVEN `reg 8 the person is a relevant person` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the answer of the rule for a relevant person` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`mlr2 for a relevant person` MEANS
    CONSIDER `reg 8 the person is a relevant person`
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN `the answer of the rule for a relevant person`
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the rule is addressed to "a relevant person" and the Regulations do not say what it does for anyone else
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN NOTHING
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether the person is a relevant person is regulation 8's answer and has not been given
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

-- A rule that applies only on a stated condition and whose text says no where the condition fails
-- ("if", "where", "provided that"): the condition is a MAYBE BOOLEAN.
GIVEN condition IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the answer when the condition holds` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`mlr2 if the condition holds` MEANS
    CONSIDER condition
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN `the answer when the condition holds`
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text's own condition failed): the caller's text introduces the condition with "if", "where" or "provided that"
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the condition has not been answered

-- A date limit written as "before" a day: strictly earlier.  The event is a MAYBE DATE: NOTHING means it has not happened.
-- When it has not happened the text has not been tested yet, so the answer is NOTHING at this helper; a caller that has a
-- later day on which the limit was missed says so itself.
GIVEN event IS A MAYBE DATE
      limit IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`mlr2 the event was before the day` MEANS
    CONSIDER event
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the event has not happened, so a limit written as "before" has been neither met nor missed
    WHEN JUST day THEN
        IF day LESS THAN limit
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text's own period elapsed): a limit written as "before" a day is missed by an event on or after that day

-- A date limit written as "on or before", "within N days" or "before the end of": on or after the limit's last day is late.
GIVEN event IS A MAYBE DATE
      `the last day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`mlr2 the event was on or before the last day` MEANS
    CONSIDER event
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the event has not happened, so the limit has been neither met nor missed
    WHEN JUST day THEN
        IF day AT MOST `the last day`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text's own period elapsed): the limit is stated in the text's own words and the event came after its last day

-- The same limit, asked on a day when the event is still outstanding: the day asked is after the last day, so the limit has been missed.
GIVEN event IS A MAYBE DATE
      `the last day` IS A DATE
      `the day asked` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`mlr2 the event was on or before the last day, asked on a day` MEANS
    CONSIDER event
    WHEN JUST day THEN `mlr2 the event was on or before the last day` (JUST day) `the last day`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN
        IF `the day asked` GREATER THAN `the last day`
        THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text's own period elapsed): the event has not happened and the last day has passed
        ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the event has not happened yet and the last day has not passed

-- A rule addressed to a described class or occasion ("The trustees of a taxable relevant trust which ...", "When ... enters into ...")
-- answers for that class or occasion and says nothing outside it.  The caller says in a comment which words limit the rule.
GIVEN condition IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`mlr2 within the scope` MEANS
    CONSIDER condition
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the caller's rule is addressed to a described class or occasion and says nothing outside it
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): an answer the scope depends on has not been given

-- The later of two days ("within 90 days of the trust being set up or, if later, ...").
GIVEN first IS A DATE
      second IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`mlr2 the later of two days` MEANS
    IF first AT LEAST second THEN first ELSE second
